Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Stylized storybook animation inspired by early twentieth-century painted illustrations.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow glows beyond the windows. A fat, bunchy VE​​LVETEEN RABBIT sits atop a stocking with holly between his paws. His coat is spotted brown and white, with thread whiskers, pink-lined ears, boot-button eyes, and a soft body made all in one piece.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a Velveteen Rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

A six-year-old BOY with brown curls, a round face, and a white nightshirt lifts the Rabbit from among nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse.

The Boy hugs him, races him across the floor, and flies him through the air.

Aunts and Uncles arrive bearing parcels. Tissue paper rustles. New toys surround the Boy.

The Rabbit slips unnoticed beneath a chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. Then, in the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The Rabbit lies on the floor beside costly mechanical toys. The clockwork mouse buzzes past. A model boat displays its rigging. A jointed wooden lion turns his painted face away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Being only made of velveteen, some of the more expensive toys quite snubbed him. Between them all, the poor little Rabbit was made to feel very insignificant and commonplace.

Beside the fender rests SKIN HORSE, an old brown toy horse, bald in patches, with visible seams and only a few hairs left in his tail.

The Rabbit nestles beside him.

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

The Skin Horse regards his worn coat without shame.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out, and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But once you are Real, you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

RABBIT
I suppose you are Real?

The Rabbit immediately lowers his ears, afraid he has caused offense. The Skin Horse smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit touches his spotless fur and polished eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He longed to become Real, and yet the idea of growing shabby and losing his eyes and whiskers was rather sad.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, a sturdy grey-haired woman in a dark dress and white apron, sweeps toys into the cupboard.

The Boy searches beneath his bed and inside a toy chest.

BOY
Nana, I can't find the china dog.

Nana glances at the clock, reaches into the cupboard, and drags the Rabbit out by one ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts the Rabbit into the Boy's arms. The Boy hugs him and carries him away.

The Skin Horse watches from the cupboard as its door closes.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A night-light burns on the mantelpiece. The Boy, now in a pale blue nightshirt, squeezes the Rabbit tight beneath his blankets.

The Boy rolls onto him, pushes him under the pillow, then pulls him out and builds tunnels through the bedclothes.

BOY
These are burrows, Bunny. Real rabbits live in burrows.

The Rabbit peers through the tunnel as the Boy crawls after him.

Night after night passes in gentle glimpses. The Boy whispers games, kisses the Rabbit's pink nose, and sleeps with both hands clasped around him.

The Rabbit's velvet thins. His tail comes partly unsewn. The pink rubs from his nose.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Very soon he grew to like it. When the Boy dropped off to sleep, the Rabbit snuggled beneath his little warm chin and dreamed there all night long.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring sunlight fills the garden. The Boy pushes the Rabbit in a wheelbarrow, picnics with him on the grass, and builds a tiny hut beneath the raspberry canes.

The Boy digs burrows in the flower bed and plunges the Rabbit into them. Earth stains the Rabbit's worn coat.

Later, the empty garden darkens under evening dew. The Rabbit lies forgotten on the lawn.

Nana approaches with a candle, finds him wet through, and rubs him with the corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny. Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy sits up and stretches out both hands.

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana places the Rabbit in his arms.

The Rabbit's dull boot-button eyes catch the night-light. Love and wonder transform his shabby face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him at last. The Boy himself had said it.

Morning sunlight enters. Nana picks up the Rabbit and pauses at the wisdom in his worn face.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - DUSK

It is summer. The Boy makes the Rabbit a nest among tall green bracken, then runs between the trees to pick flowers.

Ants travel between the Rabbit's velvet paws.

Two living rabbits emerge: BRACKEN, lean and brown, and THISTLE, small and grey. Their noses twitch, their fur ripples, and their bodies stretch and bunch as they move.

BRACKEN
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

Bracken hops sideways and rises on his hind legs.

BRACKEN
It's as easy as anything. I don't believe you can!

RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

THISTLE
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit freezes. His stuffed body has no separate hind legs.

RABBIT
I don't want to.

Bracken peers behind him.

BRACKEN
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

RABBIT
I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

BRACKEN
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

Bracken whirls and dances. The Rabbit watches, aching to join him.

RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still.

Bracken approaches until his whiskers brush the Rabbit's ear. He sniffs, flattens his ears, and recoils.

BRACKEN
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't Real!

RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Boy's footsteps approach. Bracken and Thistle vanish with a stamp of feet and a flash of white tails.

RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only the ants move. The bracken sways where the living rabbits passed.

The sun sinks. White moths flutter out. The Boy returns, gathers the Rabbit into his arms, and carries him home.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Weeks have passed. The Rabbit's whiskers are gone, his ear linings grey, his brown spots faded, and his body almost shapeless.

The Boy lies feverish beneath the blankets, his face scarlet and his curls damp. The Rabbit is pressed against his burning chest.

Nana tends the Boy while DOCTOR BELL, a grave middle-aged physician in a black coat, examines him.

The Rabbit hides beneath the bedclothes whenever they approach.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Through it all the little Velveteen Rabbit never stirred. He was afraid that someone might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

A lamp burns through another night. The Boy tosses and murmurs in his sleep.

The Rabbit creeps close to the pillow.

RABBIT
When you are well, we shall play in the raspberry thicket. We shall make fairy huts and splendid burrows.

The Boy's breathing gradually eases. His hand settles around the Rabbit.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Bright air enters through open windows. The recovering Boy sits wrapped in a shawl on the balcony outside.

The Rabbit lies tangled among the bedclothes, listening as Nana and Doctor Bell inspect the room.

DOCTOR BELL
The room must be disinfected. All the books and toys that he played with in bed must be burnt.

The Rabbit's face brightens as he watches the Boy beyond the window.

RABBIT
To-morrow we shall go to the seaside.

Nana pulls back the bedclothes and finds him.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

Doctor Bell lifts the Rabbit with two fingers.

DOCTOR BELL
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit is dropped into a sack with old picture-books and rubbish.

From the balcony, the Boy looks toward the bright horizon, unaware as Nana carries the sack away.

EXT. BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The open sack lies beside a cold bonfire heap. Beyond it, the raspberry thicket shivers in the moonlight.

The Rabbit wriggles until his head emerges. His threadbare coat offers no warmth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of the long sunlit hours in the garden, the fairy huts, the evenings in the wood, and the wonderful day when he first knew that he was Real.

The Rabbit sees remembered flashes of the Boy pushing his wheelbarrow, building a burrow, and reaching for him from bed.

RABBIT
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear rolls down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the earth.

From that wet spot, an emerald stem pushes upward. Leaves unfurl around a golden, cup-shaped flower.

The blossom opens. FAIRY rises from it, luminous and delicate, wearing a dress of pearl and dewdrops, with flowers around her neck and in her dark flowing hair.

She gathers the Rabbit into her arms and kisses his damp nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

RABBIT
No.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, I come and turn them into Real.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to everyone.

The Fairy holds him close and rises into the moonlit air.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Silver moonlight frosts the bracken. Bracken, Thistle, and a circle of wild rabbits dance in an open glade.

The Fairy descends with the shabby Velveteen Rabbit. The wild rabbits stop and gather around.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the Rabbit and places him on the grass.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit remains still, ashamed of his shapeless body.

Something tickles his nose. Without thinking, he lifts a hind foot and scratches.

He stares at the foot. His stuffed body has become living brown fur, soft and shiny, with faint traces of his old white markings. His ears twitch. Long whiskers brush the grass.

The Rabbit leaps.

He springs higher, bounds sideways, and whirls around the glade. Bracken and Thistle race beside him.

RABBIT
At last! At last!

He stops and looks back. The Fairy has vanished, leaving only the golden flower glowing among the trees.

The Rabbit races after his new companions.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

A year has turned. Warm sunlight falls through new leaves.

The Boy, healthy again and a little taller, plays among the trees.

Bracken and the REAL RABBIT creep from the bracken to watch him. The Real Rabbit is brown, but old spotted markings remain faintly visible beneath his fur.

The Boy kneels as the rabbits peer at him.

The Real Rabbit's round black eyes and soft nose hold something familiar.

BOY (V.O.)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Real Rabbit twitches his nose, then bounds away with Bracken.

At the edge of the trees, he looks back once at the child who first helped him become Real.

The Boy smiles without knowing why.

>FADE OUT.

THE END
